## Summary

Audited the Heliosian codebase at main commit `2e37f4c` for reachable security problems. The audit found two issues in background fetching and logging, fixed them, and took review through a clean result.

Before: a vouched community email could supply a linked page or image URL that made the fetcher connect to a private service or cloud metadata address, including after DNS resolution or a redirect.
Now: the shared fetch transport checks each resolved socket address and refuses nonpublic destinations. Private linked pages and images are not imported; the sender must use a public link or the supported document path.

Before: fetch and digest logs could expose capability URLs, mail text, and model output, including malformed image or redirect targets.
Now: fetch logs retain URL origins and fixed error reasons, while digest logs retain counts without message content.

The connection-time check was chosen to cover DNS and redirect paths at one choke point. Redacted logging keeps operational outcomes without token-bearing URLs or private mail. Focused fetch, extraction, digest, and security tests passed under the repository's 30-second test limit; `go vet ./...` and `git diff --check` passed. The full `internal/db` suite exceeded the per-run limit, so affected tests were selected. The final review found no significant issues. Public `/open/share/` cards and previews were left untouched because the project explicitly makes them public by ID. Main had not advanced when the final merge turn ran, so `git merge --no-ff --no-commit main` reported already up to date.

